OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Universal-Computation-with-Eventually-Stationary-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026", "title": "Universal Computation with Eventually Stationary Navier–Stokes Forcing", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Universal-Computation-with-Eventually-Stationary-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf", "family": "376", "family_title": "Universal computation in forced Navier–Stokes flows", "abstract_html": "At every fixed positive computable viscosity, we construct smooth mean-zero forces on the flat three-torus that become stationary after time one and make a fixed particle, initially in a fluid at rest, enter a fixed open set exactly when a prescribed Turing machine halts. The force has bounded derivatives of every order and a finite effective description. A reversible recording table is realized on whole planar rectangles by Hamiltonian motions, then driven by a mean-zero spatial clock. Separate choices give periodic forcing from time zero or a force whose derivatives are square-integrable in time.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Eventually steady forcing — original Theorem 1.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Eventually steady forcing — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\) and \\(x=(X,Y,Z)\\in\\mathbb T^3\\), with the flat metric and componentwise flat Laplacian.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive computable viscosity \\(\\nu\\). Consider\n  \\[\n  \\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=-\\nabla p+\\nu\\Delta u+f,\n  \\qquad \\operatorname{div}u=0,\\qquad u(0)=0. \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n  Pressure has zero spatial mean.</p><p class=\"li\">A classical solution has continuous \\(u,u_t\\), spatial derivatives of \\(u\\) through order two, \\(p\\), and \\(\\nabla p\\) on every closed finite time cylinder.</p><p class=\"li\">For a particle label \\(a\\), its material trajectory satisfies\n  \\[\n  \\dot\\Phi(t,a)=u(t,\\Phi(t,a)),\\qquad \\Phi(0,a)=a.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">An effective smooth force is supplied by a finite program evaluating every requested mixed derivative at computably supplied space-time arguments to any prescribed rational error, and computing the derivative bounds used in the construction. The program must describe all branches of the mechanism; it cannot first compute the selected execution and replay it.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  P=(1/4,1/4,0),\\qquad\n  \\mathcal O=\\{(X,Y,Z)\\in\\mathbb T^3:1/32&lt;Y&lt;1/8\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an effective procedure taking a deterministic Turing machine and finite input to a body force \\(f\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(f\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb T^3\\times[0,\\infty))\\), has zero spatial mean, and is independent of time for \\(t\\geq1\\). Every spatial, temporal, and mixed derivative of \\(f\\) is bounded on this entire domain.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Equation (1) has a global smooth solution, unique in the classical class on every finite time interval. Its kinetic energy is bounded uniformly in time.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The material trajectory from \\(P\\) enters \\(\\mathcal O\\) at a finite time if and only if the machine halts on the input.</p><p>The force is specified by a finite formula which does not simulate the chosen execution. The construction is uniform as a formula in an arbitrary positive parameter \\(\\nu\\). For a fixed positive noncomputable \\(\\nu\\), the same finite formulas and mathematical conclusions hold, with evaluation relative to that parameter.</p><p>The force may also be made solenoidal by the periodic Helmholtz–Leray projection, changing only the pressure. This result concerns explicitly supplied smooth solutions, not regularity for arbitrary fluid data. The reachability equivalence concerns exact trajectories and supplies no uniform finite-precision tolerance for an infinite encoded computation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Construct \\(W\\) by Proposition 6. Use the fixed ramp\n\\[\n\\alpha(t)=\\beta(2t-1),\n\\qquad\nU(t,X,Y,Z)=\\alpha(t)W(X,Y,Z), \\tag{13}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\beta\\) is supplied by Proposition 1. Prescribe\n\\[\nf(t)=\n\\alpha'(t)W+\n\\alpha(t)^2(W\\cdot\\nabla)W-\n\\nu\\alpha(t)\\Delta W. \\tag{14}\n\\]\nEvery term on the right is a previously constructed function, not an unknown solution.</p><p>The ramp is zero near zero and equals one for \\(t\\geq1\\). Thus \\(U(0)=0\\), the force is eventually stationary, and every mixed derivative is bounded. The force has zero mean: \\(W\\) and \\(\\Delta W\\) have zero mean, while periodicity and \\(\\operatorname{div}W=0\\) give\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb T^3}(W\\cdot\\nabla)W\\,dx=0.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 2 gives the exact solution\n\\[\nu=U,\\qquad p=0,\n\\]\nand uniqueness in the classical class on every finite interval. Since \\(U\\) is uniformly bounded on the compact spatial domain, its kinetic energy is uniformly bounded.</p><p>Material trajectories of \\(U\\) are trajectories of \\(W\\) with path parameter\n\\[\ns(t)=\\int_0^t\\alpha(r)\\,dr.\n\\]\nThis function is continuous, finite at finite \\(t\\), and has range \\([0,\\infty)\\). Indeed, the identity\n\\[\n\\beta(u)+\\beta(1-u)=1\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\ns(1)=\\frac14,\n\\qquad\ns(t)=t-\\frac34\\quad(t\\geq1).\n\\]\nConsequently, every positive integer computational phase \\(n\\) occurs at physical time \\(n+3/4\\). Proposition 6 transfers the reachability equivalence to the physical material flow, including all intermediate times.</p><p>The force formula uses only the finite rule table, the finite input’s rational initial coordinates, and the finite routing construction. Every branch is implemented in each phase cycle; the current fluid position selects the next branch. Evaluating the force at a late physical time requires no evaluation of an equally long machine run, and after time one the force has no time-dependent instruction stream. This proves effectiveness and the absence of external replay. Taking any fixed universal Turing machine gives universal encoding.</p><p>The formulas are uniform in the positive parameter \\(\\nu\\). For noncomputable \\(\\nu\\), their evaluation is relative to that parameter, while the mathematical conclusions remain unchanged.</p><p>Finally, Proposition 3 permits replacement by an effective mean-zero solenoidal force, changing only the pressure and preserving eventual stationarity and every mixed-derivative bound. This is the eventually stationary part of original Remark 5.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Periodic forcing from time zero — original Corollary 5.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Periodic forcing from time zero — original Corollary 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive computable viscosity \\(\\nu\\), a deterministic Turing machine, and finite input.</p><p class=\"li\">Use equation (1), zero initial velocity, the definition of effective smooth force, and the fixed particle \\(P\\) and open set \\(\\mathcal O\\) from Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an effective construction on \\(\\mathbb T^3\\) of a smooth mean-zero force of period one in time, with every mixed derivative bounded, whose unique global classical velocity has the following property:\n\\[\n\\Phi(t,P)\\in\\mathcal O\\text{ for some finite }t\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\text{the machine halts on the input}.\n\\]</p><p>The force may also be replaced by an effective mean-zero solenoidal force without changing the velocity or particle event.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the field \\(V\\) of Proposition 5 and define directly\n\\[\nU(t,X,Y,Z)=\n\\bigl(V_1(t,X,Y),V_2(t,X,Y),0\\bigr).\n\\]\nSince \\(V\\) vanishes near phase zero,\n\\[\nU(0)=0.\n\\]\nThe velocity is divergence free, mean zero, and \\(1\\)-periodic. Define its residual force\n\\[\nf=\\partial_tU+(U\\cdot\\nabla)U-\\nu\\Delta U.\n\\]\nThe force has the same period, zero mean, and bounded mixed derivatives.</p><p>Proposition 2 gives the unique global classical solution \\(U\\). The particle trajectory from \\((1/4,1/4,0)\\) is exactly the planar processor trajectory with third coordinate zero. Proposition 5 proves the fixed-event equivalence. No loading interval is required.</p><p>Proposition 3 permits replacement by an effective mean-zero solenoidal force, preserving periodicity, mixed-derivative bounds, velocity, and particle event. This is the periodic part of original Remark 5.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Scalar clock — original Proposition 6.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Scalar clock — original Proposition 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive computable viscosity \\(\\nu\\), a deterministic Turing machine, and finite input.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W\\) be the autonomous spatial field constructed for Theorem 1. Explicitly, if \\(V(s,X,Y)\\) is the smooth periodic planar processor and \\(\\chi_*(X,Y)\\) is a smooth cutoff equal to one near\n  \\[\n  J_*=[1/8,7/8]\\times[1/32,7/8]\n  \\]\n  and compactly supported in the open unit-square chart, then\n  \\[\n  g(X,Y)=\\partial_X\\bigl(X\\chi_*(X,Y)\\bigr),\n  \\qquad\n  W(X,Y,Z)=\\bigl(V_1(Z,X,Y),V_2(Z,X,Y),g(X,Y)\\bigr).\n  \\]\n  Compactly supported chart functions are extended periodically.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(a:[0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)\\) is computably smooth, zero near zero, and has an effective finite bound for \\(\\lvert a^{(h)}\\rvert\\) for every integer \\(h\\geq0\\), including boundedness of \\(a\\) itself. Assume\n  \\[\n  \\int_0^\\infty a(t)\\,dt=\\infty.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  U_a(t,x)=a(t)W(x),\n  \\qquad\n  F_a=a'W+a^2(W\\cdot\\nabla)W-\\nu a\\Delta W.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The force \\(F_a\\) is smooth and mean zero, has bounded mixed derivatives, and produces zero initial velocity and the same fixed-particle event as Theorem 1. Its unique classical velocity is \\(U_a\\).</p><p>For\n\\[\na(t)=\\frac{\\beta(2t-1)}{1+t},\n\\qquad\n\\beta(r)=\\frac{E(r)}{E(r)+E(1-r)},\n\\qquad\nE(r)=\n\\begin{cases}\ne^{-1/r},&amp;r&gt;0,\\\\\n0,&amp;r\\leq0,\n\\end{cases} \\tag{15}\n\\]\nevery time order \\(h\\geq0\\) and spatial multi-index \\(\\mu\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\|\\partial_t^h\\partial_x^\\mu U_a(t)\\|_\\infty\n+\n\\|\\partial_t^h\\partial_x^\\mu F_a(t)\\|_\\infty\n\\leq C_{h,\\mu}(1+t)^{-1-h}.\n\\]\nIn particular, all these spatial supremum norms belong to \\(L^2(0,\\infty)\\), and the force tends uniformly to zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>The field \\(W\\) has the smoothness, derivative bounds, divergence-free property, zero mean, and computational trajectory supplied by Proposition 6.</p><p>The residual formula is (14) with \\(\\alpha=a\\):\n\\[\nF_a=a'W+a^2(W\\cdot\\nabla)W-\\nu a\\Delta W.\n\\]\nSince \\(a\\) is zero near zero, \\(U_a(0)=0\\). Proposition 2 gives the solution \\(U_a\\) and uniqueness. The mean-zero and mixed-derivative assertions follow by differentiating the three coefficient functions \\(a'\\), \\(a^2\\), and \\(a\\).</p><p>If \\(\\gamma(s)\\) is the trajectory of \\(W\\) from \\(P\\), then\n\\[\n\\gamma(s_a(t)),\n\\qquad\ns_a(t)=\\int_0^t a(r)\\,dr,\n\\]\nsolves the material equation for \\(U_a\\). This clock is continuous, nondecreasing, finite at every finite time, and unbounded. The intermediate value theorem gives\n\\[\ns_a([0,\\infty))=[0,\\infty).\n\\]\nThus the reachability event is unchanged.</p><p>For the specific profile (15),\n\\[\na(t)=(1+t)^{-1}\\qquad(t\\geq1),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ns_a(t)=s_a(1)+\\log\\frac{1+t}{2}\n\\qquad(t\\geq1).\n\\]\nHence the clock is onto.</p><p>The coefficients \\(a'\\) and \\(a^2\\) have time derivatives of order \\(h\\) bounded by a constant times \\((1+t)^{-2-h}\\), whereas \\(a^{(h)}\\) is bounded by a constant times \\((1+t)^{-1-h}\\). Every derivative of the fixed spatial fields is bounded. These facts give\n\\[\n\\|\\partial_t^h\\partial_x^\\mu U_a(t)\\|_\\infty\n+\n\\|\\partial_t^h\\partial_x^\\mu F_a(t)\\|_\\infty\n\\leq C_{h,\\mu}(1+t)^{-1-h}\n\\]\nfor \\(t\\geq1\\). Compactness gives an enlarged constant on \\([0,1]\\). Squaring and integrating proves the \\(L^2\\) assertion, and the estimate with \\(h=0\\), \\(\\mu=0\\) gives uniform convergence of the force to zero.</p><p>This slow profile and the eventually stationary profile are different forces. An integrable nonnegative scalar clock would traverse only a bounded amount of the same spatial trajectory and cannot replace the onto-clock hypothesis. The exact positional encoding and finite-time tube estimate do not give a uniform perturbation tolerance for an unbounded computation.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Undecidable fixed-particle reachability — original Corollary 6.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Undecidable fixed-particle reachability — original Corollary 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive computable viscosity \\(\\nu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider the finite force descriptions produced by Theorem 1, with zero initial velocity and the fixed particle \\(P\\) and fixed open set \\(\\mathcal O\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>No algorithm decides, for every such finite force description, whether the material trajectory from \\(P\\) enters \\(\\mathcal O\\) at a finite time.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose an algorithm decided the particle event for all finite force descriptions constructed in Theorem 1. Compose it with the terminating construction of the force from a machine and finite input. The event equivalence would decide whether an arbitrary machine halts on a finite input.</p><p>It would therefore decide Turing’s symbol-printing problem: simulate a given machine and halt exactly when its specified symbol is printed; if it stops without printing, continue forever. Turing proves that no such decision procedure exists. Hence the proposed event algorithm cannot exist.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Effective flat profiles — original Lemma 2.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Effective flat profiles — original Lemma 2.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  E(t)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  e^{-1/t},&amp;t&gt;0,\\\\\n  0,&amp;t\\leq0,\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\qquad\n  \\beta(t)=\\frac{E(t)}{E(t)+E(1-t)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Computable smoothness means effective evaluation of every derivative at computably supplied arguments to any prescribed rational error.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functions \\(E\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are computably smooth. The function \\(\\beta\\) is zero for \\(t\\leq0\\), one for \\(t\\geq1\\), and satisfies\n\\[\n\\beta(t)+\\beta(1-t)=1.\n\\]\nProducts of rational translates and rescalings give effective cutoffs equal to one near any prescribed rational closed box and supported in any prescribed larger open rational box. All derivatives of these cutoffs have effective bounds.</p><p>Periodizations of smooth functions supported away from the endpoints of a unit interval, and coordinatewise periodizations of compact chart-supported fields, can be evaluated effectively without an integer-part test on a computably supplied real argument.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(t&gt;0\\), the \\(j\\)-th derivative of \\(E\\) is\n\\[\nP_j(1/t)e^{-1/t},\n\\]\nwhere the polynomial \\(P_j\\) is generated recursively. For integers \\(m,k\\geq0\\) and \\(y&gt;0\\),\n\\[\ny^m e^{-y}\\leq(m+k)!\\,y^{-k}.\n\\]\nThese estimates prove flatness and give an effective error tending to zero as \\(t\\downarrow0\\). Away from zero, ordinary arithmetic and the exponential evaluate the derivatives.</p><p>The denominator defining \\(\\beta\\) is at least \\(e^{-2}\\), since at least one of \\(t\\) and \\(1-t\\) is at least \\(1/2\\). Quotient differentiation is therefore effective even when equality with an endpoint is undecidable.</p><p>For \\(a&lt;b&lt;c&lt;d\\), the product\n\\[\n\\beta\\!\\left(\\frac{t-a}{b-a}\\right)\n\\beta\\!\\left(\\frac{d-t}{d-c}\\right)\n\\]\nhas the required interval plateau; coordinate products give boxes. The derivative bounds follow by the product and chain rules and the preceding estimates.</p><p>For periodic evaluation, let \\(F_0\\) be smooth on \\([0,1]\\), zero near its endpoints, and extended by zero to \\(\\mathbb R\\). Given a computably supplied \\(s\\in\\mathbb R\\), compute a rational \\(q\\) with \\(\\lvert q-s\\rvert&lt;1\\). Then\n\\[\nF_{\\mathrm{per}}^{(j)}(s)\n=\n\\sum_{k=\\lfloor q\\rfloor-2}^{\\lceil q\\rceil+2}F_0^{(j)}(s-k).\n\\]\nEvery omitted translate is zero, since a nonzero term requires \\(k\\in[s-1,s]\\). Floors and ceilings are taken only of the rational \\(q\\), so no integer-part test is made on \\(s\\). The same coordinatewise finite sum evaluates spatial periodizations of compact chart-supported fields.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Prescribed velocity and comparison — original Lemma 2.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Prescribed velocity and comparison — original Lemma 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\nu&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\) be a smooth divergence-free velocity on \\([0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb T^3\\), with \\(U(0)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  F=U_t+(U\\cdot\\nabla)U-\\nu\\Delta U. \\tag{2}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use equation (1), zero-mean pressure, and the classical regularity specified in Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The pair \\((U,0)\\) is a global smooth solution with force \\(F\\), unique in the classical class on every finite time interval. There is a unique material flow through every finite time. If \\(U\\) has zero spatial mean, then \\(F\\) has zero spatial mean.</p>", "proof": "<p>Substitution proves existence.</p><p>For another solution \\((v,p)\\) with the same force and data, set \\(w=v-U\\). Its equation is\n\\[\nw_t+(v\\cdot\\nabla)w+(w\\cdot\\nabla)U-\\nu\\Delta w+\\nabla p=0.\n\\]\nThe stated regularity permits multiplication by \\(w\\) and periodic integration by parts. The pressure and transport terms vanish, giving\n\\[\n\\frac12\\frac{d}{dt}\\|w\\|_2^2+\\nu\\|\\nabla w\\|_2^2\n\\leq\n\\|\\nabla U\\|_{\\infty,\\mathrm{op}}\\|w\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nOn every finite interval the coefficient is bounded. Gronwall’s inequality and \\(w(0)=0\\) give \\(w=0\\). The pressure gradient then vanishes, and pressure normalization gives \\(p=0\\). This is the classical difference-energy method cited in the paper from Leray.</p><p>Smoothness on compact time cylinders gives a unique material flow through every finite time.</p><p>Finally, each component of \\((U\\cdot\\nabla)U\\) is the divergence of a periodic vector field, and the integral of \\(\\Delta U\\) vanishes. Integrating (2) proves the mean assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Solenoidal projection — original Lemma 2.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Solenoidal projection — original Lemma 2.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be a computably smooth mean-zero force on \\(\\mathbb T^3\\), possibly depending on time, with effective spatial derivative bounds.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\varphi\\) be the zero-mean solution of\n  \\[\n  \\Delta\\varphi=\\operatorname{div}F,\n  \\qquad\n  \\int_{\\mathbb T^3}\\varphi\\,dx=0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The force \\(F\\) can be replaced by the computably smooth mean-zero solenoidal force \\(F-\\nabla\\varphi\\), while replacing pressure \\(p\\) by \\(p-\\varphi\\). The velocity is unchanged.</p><p>Eventual stationarity, periodicity, and bounds for mixed derivatives are preserved. Temporal \\(L^2\\) estimates for all spatial derivative orders are preserved as well.</p>", "proof": "<p>In the Fourier basis \\(e^{2\\pi i k\\cdot x}\\), set\n\\[\n\\widehat\\varphi(k)\n=\n-\\frac{i k\\cdot\\widehat F(k)}{2\\pi|k|^2}\n\\quad(k\\neq0),\n\\qquad\n\\widehat\\varphi(0)=0.\n\\]\nThe multiplier for \\(\\nabla\\varphi\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac{kk^{\\mathsf T}}{|k|^2},\n\\]\nwhich has operator norm one.</p><p>For derivative order \\(r\\), integrate the coefficient of \\(F\\) by parts \\(r+5\\) times in a coordinate with largest \\(\\lvert k_i\\rvert\\). There are at most \\(Cn^2\\) lattice points with \\(n\\leq|k|&lt;n+1\\), so the derivative series converges absolutely with a computable tail. In particular,\n\\[\n\\|\\partial_t^h\\partial_x^\\mu\\nabla\\varphi(t)\\|_\\infty\n\\leq\nC_\\mu\\max_i\n\\|\\partial_t^h\\partial_{x_i}^{|\\mu|+5}F(t)\\|_\\infty.\n\\]\nThis proves the uniform and temporal bounds.</p><p>Riemann sums with derivative error bounds evaluate each Fourier coefficient; the series tail makes the procedure effective. Differentiation in time commutes with this fixed spatial multiplier. Finally,\n\\[\n-\\nabla(p-\\varphi)+(F-\\nabla\\varphi)=-\\nabla p+F,\n\\]\nwhich checks the pressure sign and proves that the velocity is unchanged.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**An injective local compiler — original Lemma 3.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**An injective local compiler — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A deterministic machine has one head on a tape indexed by \\(\\mathbb Z\\), a finite alphabet containing a blank, and head moves in \\(\\{-1,0,1\\}\\). A transition writes one symbol before moving.</p><p class=\"li\">The finite input occupies cells \\(0,\\ldots,r-1\\), all other cells are blank, and the head starts at \\(0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Effectively put the machine in the convention of a single halting state \\(q_H\\), no rules there, and a total table elsewhere. Missing instructions and multiple halting states are redirected to a new halting state.</p><p class=\"li\">Add a fresh initial state \\(q_{\\mathrm{start}}\\), whose rules leave the symbol and head unchanged and enter the former initial state. This preserves halting even when the former initial state was halting.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>From the machine and input one can effectively construct a finite partial one-head transition table and an initial tape differing from a constant blank tape at finitely many cells, with these properties:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Its initialized run reaches a single terminal state, with no outgoing rules, if and only if the original machine halts. Otherwise every successive transition is defined and the run continues forever. At specified finite checkpoints, its state and tape recover the original computation.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. All rules arriving at a fixed state have the same head displacement. The destination state and the full written symbol determine the incoming rule, including its old state and read symbol.</p><p>Consequently, the full configuration map is injective on its domain.</p>", "proof": "<p>Construct the finite alphabet, states, table, and finitely supported initial tape of Lemma 4.1. That lemma proves the initialized simulation and halting equivalence. Lemma 4.2 proves the displacement property, local inverse, and injectivity of the full configuration map.</p><p>All expansions are finite and effective. The construction retains instruction history rather than importing a reversible-machine existence theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**The seven-row recording table and its initialized run.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**The seven-row recording table and its initialized run.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q,\\Gamma\\) be the states and alphabet of the augmented machine in Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">For\n  \\[\n  \\tau=(q,a)\\in\\mathcal T=(Q\\setminus\\{q_H\\})\\times\\Gamma,\n  \\]\n  write the machine instruction as\n  \\[\n  (q_\\tau^+,a_\\tau^+,d_\\tau).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the three-track alphabet\n  \\[\n  \\Sigma=\\Gamma\\times\\{0,1\\}\\times\\mathcal L,\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal L=\\{\\bot,E\\}\\sqcup\\mathcal T.\n  \\]\n  The tracks hold data, a head marker, and history.</p><p class=\"li\">The states are \\(R_q,C_q,L_q\\) for \\(q\\in Q\\), and \\(A_\\tau,F_\\tau\\) for \\(\\tau\\in\\mathcal T\\), all distinguished by type and subscript. Only \\(R_{q_H}\\) is terminal.</p><p class=\"li\">Initially, the state is \\(R_{q_{\\mathrm{start}}}\\), the data and head are the given data and head, all marker bits vanish, and history is \\(E\\) at cell \\(2\\) and \\(\\bot\\) elsewhere. The full blank symbol is \\((\\mathrm{blank},0,\\bot)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the entire partial table by expanding the following rows over all symbols satisfying their restrictions, with \\(a\\in\\Gamma\\) and \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{lll}\n  (R_q,(a,0,\\ell))\n  &amp;\\mapsto(A_\\tau,(a_\\tau^+,0,\\ell),d_\\tau)\n  &amp;q\\neq q_H,\\ \\tau=(q,a),\\ \\ell\\neq E,\\\\\n  (A_\\tau,(a,0,\\ell))\n  &amp;\\mapsto(F_\\tau,(a,1,\\ell),1)\n  &amp;\\ell\\neq E,\\\\\n  (F_\\tau,(a,0,\\ell))\n  &amp;\\mapsto(F_\\tau,(a,0,\\ell),1)\n  &amp;\\ell\\neq E,\\\\\n  (F_\\tau,(a,0,E))\n  &amp;\\mapsto(C_{q_\\tau^+},(a,0,\\tau),1),&amp;\\\\\n  (C_q,(a,0,\\bot))\n  &amp;\\mapsto(L_q,(a,0,E),-1),&amp;\\\\\n  (L_q,(a,0,\\ell))\n  &amp;\\mapsto(L_q,(a,0,\\ell),-1)\n  &amp;\\ell\\neq E,\\\\\n  (L_q,(a,1,\\ell))\n  &amp;\\mapsto(R_q,(a,0,\\ell),0)\n  &amp;\\ell\\neq E.\n  \\end{array} \\tag{3}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At the ready checkpoint after \\(n\\) augmented steps, the control is \\(R_q\\), and its data and head \\(h\\) are those of the augmented machine, with \\(\\lvert h\\rvert\\leq n\\). Every marker bit vanishes. History cells \\(2,\\ldots,n+1\\) contain the successive \\(n\\) instruction tags, cell \\(n+2\\) contains \\(E\\), and every other history cell contains \\(\\bot\\).</p><p>The initialized run reaches \\(R_{q_H}\\) exactly when the original machine halts. If the original machine does not halt, the compiled run continues forever without encountering an undefined rule.</p>", "proof": "<p>The invariant holds initially.</p><p>For its induction step, suppose the ready checkpoint after \\(n\\) augmented steps has head \\(h\\), and the next instruction is \\(\\tau\\). The first row performs the data instruction and moves to\n\\[\nh'=h+d_\\tau\\leq n+1&lt;n+2.\n\\]\nThe second row marks \\(h'\\) and starts a rightward scan. The third and fourth rows reach the finite frontier \\(n+2\\), replace \\(E\\) by \\(\\tau\\), and advance one cell. The fifth row writes the new frontier at \\(n+3\\) and turns left. The last two rows return to the unique marker, erase it, and enter \\(R_{q_\\tau^+}\\) at \\(h'\\).</p><p>This takes\n\\[\n4+2(n+2-h')\n\\]\ntransitions, a positive finite number. No undefined rule is encountered. The invariant, halting equivalence, and checkpoint correspondence follow.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Local inverse of the recording table.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Local inverse of the recording table.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the alphabet, states, and expanded partial table (3) of Lemma 4.1.</p><p class=\"li\">A full configuration consists of its state, head position, and entire tape.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>All rules arriving at a fixed state have the same displacement. The destination state and full written symbol identify the unique incoming expanded rule.</p><p>The full configuration map is injective on its domain.</p>", "proof": "<p>Check destination by destination.</p><p class=\"li\">Into \\(A_\\tau\\), the tag fixes the old state and data symbol, and the written symbol retains the old history letter.</p><p class=\"li\">Into \\(F_\\tau\\), both incoming types move right. Arrival from \\(A_\\tau\\) writes marker \\(1\\), whereas the scan loop writes marker \\(0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Into \\(C_q\\), the move is right, the written tag identifies \\(\\tau\\), and the data letter is retained.</p><p class=\"li\">Into \\(L_q\\), both types move left. Arrival from \\(C_q\\) writes \\(E\\), whereas the loop never does.</p><p class=\"li\">Into \\(R_q\\), only the last row applies, with displacement zero and the data and history letters retained.</p><p>In every case, the destination and written full symbol recover the unique expanded rule.</p><p>For a destination configuration, its state fixes the displacement \\(d\\), so the old head was at the new head minus \\(d\\). The symbol at that cell identifies the rule and hence the old read symbol and state. Restoring that one symbol recovers the entire predecessor configuration.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**A smooth planar processor — original Theorem 4.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**A smooth planar processor — original Theorem 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Start with a deterministic Turing machine and finite input, and compile them by Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(p=(1/4,1/4)\\in\\mathbb T^2\\), and define\n  \\[\n  J_*=[1/8,7/8]\\times[1/32,7/8],\n  \\qquad\n  H=\\{(X,Y)\\in\\mathbb T^2:1/32&lt;Y&lt;1/8\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonautonomous planar field \\(V\\), write \\(\\Phi^V\\) for its transition maps.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can effectively construct a smooth field \\(V(s,X,Y)\\) on \\(\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb T^2\\) with the following properties.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(V\\) is \\(1\\)-periodic in \\(s\\), vanishes on neighborhoods of integer \\(s\\), is divergence free in \\((X,Y)\\), and has zero spatial mean. Its planar support is a compact subset of the open unit-square chart. It has a smooth periodic Hamiltonian potential supported in that chart. The same formula, extended by zero, gives a compactly supported field on \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) with the same conclusions below.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The construction supplies a finite list of closed rational source rectangles \\(D_r\\), target rectangles \\(H_r\\), and affine maps\n   \\[\n   F_r:D_r\\to H_r\n   \\]\n   with positive reciprocal diagonal linear parts. The source rectangles are pairwise disjoint, and independently the target rectangles are pairwise disjoint. These maps implement the symbolic branches under an explicit tape encoding. The period map agrees with \\(F_r\\) on a positive neighborhood of \\(D_r\\), with an effective lower bound for the neighborhood radius. Every derivative of the field has an effective finite uniform bound.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Starting from \\(p\\) at phase \\(s=0\\), the trajectory at successive integer times encodes every transition of the compiled machine until its terminal state is reached. All these trajectory segments lie in \\(J_*\\). The trajectory reaches \\(H\\) at a finite time exactly when the original machine halts.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. If the machine does not halt, then\n   \\[\n   3/16\\leq Y\\leq7/8\n   \\]\n   throughout the trajectory. If it halts, an integer-time point lies in \\(H\\) at distance at least \\(1/32\\) from its boundary.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. One can compute \\(L\\geq1\\) bounding both \\(D_{X,Y}V\\) and \\(D_{X,Y}^2V\\) globally in operator norm. Every planar transition map over an arbitrary phase interval of length \\(s\\geq0\\), and its inverse, has first derivative norm at most \\(e^{Ls}\\) and second derivative norm at most\n   \\[\n   e^{2Ls}-e^{Ls}.\n   \\]\n   Initial perturbations are magnified by at most \\(e^{Ls}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 4 and Lemmas 5.1–5.3. Each slot has a compactly supported Hamiltonian potential. The resulting periodic field \\(V\\) is divergence free, has zero planar mean, and vanishes near integer phase times. Its potential is supported in the open unit-square chart. Extending by zero gives the asserted field on \\(\\mathbb R^2\\).</p><p>At integer phase times, induction using (7) proves that the trajectory from \\(p\\) has exactly the coordinates of the next compiled configuration. The radix code is interior to its appropriate branch rectangle, including the left-symbol subrectangle for a left move. At ready checkpoints, Proposition 4 recovers the original state and tape.</p><p>If absolute tape indices are desired, the finite history block begins at absolute cell \\(2\\). Its tags and frontier are visible in the two head-relative stacks and locate the head relative to that cell. Thus the correspondence encodes the computation, not merely its outcome.</p><p>If the machine halts, some integer phase time reaches the terminal square. If it does not, every successive state is nonterminal, and (11) excludes \\(H\\) at all times. The smooth field is defined everywhere for all phase times, so continuation after a halting event presents no issue.</p><p>All tables, rational rectangles, orders, clearances, and slots are computed by finite procedures from the machine and input. The only additional constants are computable numbers such as \\(\\log B\\). Proposition 1 makes the cutoffs computably smooth rather than merely formal piecewise descriptions. Near a switching point, its derivative estimates give effective smallness bounds, with denominator lower bound \\(e^{-2}\\). Finite symbolic differentiation gives effective uniform bounds for every derivative of every pulse, including across joints and chart boundaries. No exact-equality test at a switching point is needed.</p><p>Choose an effective \\(L\\geq1\\) bounding \\(DV\\) and \\(D^2V\\) uniformly. The first variational equation gives\n\\[\n\\|D\\Phi_s^V\\|\\leq e^{Ls}.\n\\]\nThe second gives\n\\[\n\\|D^2\\Phi_s^V\\|\n\\leq\n\\int_0^s e^{L(s-r)}Le^{2Lr}\\,dr\n=\ne^{2Ls}-e^{Ls}\n\\leq e^{2Ls}.\n\\]</p><p>For arbitrary initial phase \\(s_0\\), the inverse transition map from \\(s_0\\) to \\(s_0+s\\) is the time-\\(s\\) map of\n\\[\n\\frac{dq}{dr}=-V(s_0+s-r,q),\n\\qquad 0\\leq r\\leq s.\n\\]\nThis reversed field has the same global spatial derivative bounds. Its variational equations give the same first- and second-derivative estimates for the inverse map, both on the torus and for the compactly supported planar extension.</p><p>The difference of two lifted trajectories satisfies the Lipschitz estimate \\(e^{Ls}\\). In particular, an initial perturbation of size \\(\\eta e^{-Ls}\\) stays within \\(\\eta\\) up to phase time \\(s\\). This is a finite-time estimate, not a uniform robustness claim for an infinite computation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Separated radix coordinates and full rectangle maps.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Separated radix coordinates and full rectangle maps.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the compiled alphabet \\(\\Sigma\\), states, initial tape, and partial rules from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(K=|\\Sigma|\\), \\(B=2K+2\\), and assign the symbols distinct odd digits\n  \\[\n  c(a)\\in\\{1,3,\\ldots,2K-1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  C(a_1,a_2,\\ldots)=\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac{c(a_k)}{B^k},\n  \\qquad\n  I_a=\\frac{c(a)+[0,1]}{B},\n  \\qquad\n  I_{a,b}=\\frac{c(a)+I_b}{B}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a compiled configuration with state \\(i\\), head \\(h\\), and tape \\((a_k)_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}\\), use\n  \\[\n  x=C(a_{h-1},a_{h-2},\\ldots),\n  \\qquad\n  y=C(a_h,a_{h+1},\\ldots). \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n  Let \\(x_0,y_0\\) be the initial coordinates.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m\\) be the number of compiled states. Let \\(N\\) count each displacement \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) rule once and each displacement \\(-1\\) rule \\(K\\) times. Set\n  \\[\n  \\lambda=\\frac1{100(m+N+1)},\n  \\qquad\n  a_*=\\frac14-\\lambda x_0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Associate to each state the square parametrization\n  \\[\n  \\Psi_i(x,y)=(a_*+\\lambda x,b_i+\\lambda y), \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n  where\n  \\[\n  b_i=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  1/4-\\lambda y_0,&amp;i=R_{q_{\\mathrm{start}}},\\\\\n  1/16,&amp;i=R_{q_H},\\\\\n  1/3+\\ell/[12(m+1)],&amp;i\\text{ is the }\\ell\\text{-th remaining state}.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For an expanded rule \\((i,a)\\mapsto(j,b,d)\\), use the following local branch rectangles and maps. For \\(d=-1\\), make one branch for every \\(e\\in\\Sigma\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n  d&amp;\\text{domain}&amp;(x',y')&amp;\\text{image}\\\\ \\hline\n  1&amp;\n  [0,1]\\times I_a&amp;\n  \\bigl((c(b)+x)/B,\\ By-c(a)\\bigr)&amp;\n  I_b\\times[0,1]\\\\{}[1mm]\n  0&amp;\n  [0,1]\\times I_a&amp;\n  \\bigl(x,\\ y+(c(b)-c(a))/B\\bigr)&amp;\n  [0,1]\\times I_b\\\\{}[1mm]\n  -1&amp;\n  I_e\\times I_a&amp;\n  \\left(Bx-c(e),\\\n  \\dfrac{c(e)}B+\\dfrac{c(b)+By-c(a)}{B^2}\\right)&amp;\n  [0,1]\\times I_{e,b}.\n  \\end{array} \\tag{7}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Map each domain by \\(\\Psi_i\\) and each image by \\(\\Psi_j\\). Enumerate the actual pairs as \\((D_r,H_r)\\), \\(1\\leq r\\leq N\\), with centers \\(\\mathbf d_r,\\mathbf h_r\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The sequence representation is unique, and every sequence code is interior to its first-symbol interval. The initial coordinates \\(x_0,y_0\\) are computable rationals, and the initialized planar code is \\(p=(1/4,1/4)\\).</p><p>The state squares are pairwise disjoint. Every nonterminal state square satisfies\n\\[\n1/4-\\lambda\\leq Y&lt;1/2, \\tag{6}\n\\]\nwhereas the terminal square satisfies\n\\[\n1/16\\leq Y\\leq1/16+\\lambda.\n\\]</p><p>The rectangles \\(D_r,H_r\\) have rational endpoints and side lengths at most \\(\\lambda\\). The \\(D_r\\) are pairwise disjoint, and the \\(H_r\\) are pairwise disjoint; no disjointness between the two families is required. Their branch maps implement the tape rules and have the form\n\\[\nF_r(\\mathbf w)=\n\\mathbf h_r+A_r(\\mathbf w-\\mathbf d_r),\n\\qquad\nA_r=\\operatorname{diag}(\\mu_r,\\mu_r^{-1}),\n\\qquad\n\\mu_r=B^{-d}. \\tag{8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Every sequence code lies in\n\\[\n\\left[\\frac1{B-1},\\frac{2K-1}{B-1}\\right]\\subset(0,1).\n\\]\nThe closed intervals \\(I_a\\) have positive gaps, as do the intervals \\(I_{a,b}\\) for distinct ordered pairs. A sequence code is interior to its first-symbol interval. Reading that interval and rescaling recovers the tail, proving uniqueness.</p><p>The initial coordinates are computable rationals: the finite nonblank part is followed on each side by a geometric constant-symbol tail. Formula (5) sends the initial code to \\((1/4,1/4)\\).</p><p>Consecutive squares in the last group are separated because\n\\[\n\\lambda&lt;\\frac1{12(m+1)}.\n\\]\nThe initial and terminal squares occupy separate lower height ranges. The stated height bounds follow.</p><p>In (7), a right move prefixes the written symbol to the left stack and removes the read symbol from the right stack. A left move removes \\(e\\) from the left stack and prefixes \\(e,b\\) to the old right tail. The stationary case replaces the first right-stack digit. These are exactly the tape operations, and every displayed map has determinant one.</p><p>The source rectangles are pairwise disjoint by determinism, state separation, and digit gaps. For target rectangles, Proposition 4 says that a target state fixes the displacement and that distinct incoming rules have distinct written full symbols. For displacements \\(1\\) and \\(0\\), these symbols separate the \\(I_b\\) factors. For displacement \\(-1\\), the ordered pairs \\((e,b)\\) separate the \\(I_{e,b}\\) factors.</p><p>Conjugating the local branch maps by the state parametrizations gives (8). Their endpoints are rational, and each side has length at most \\(\\lambda\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Localized Hamiltonian translations and reciprocal scalings.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Localized Hamiltonian translations and reciprocal scalings.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\([s_0,s_1]\\) be a phase slot, with \\(s_0&lt;s_1\\), and use \\(\\beta\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\theta(s)=\n  \\beta\\!\\left(\\frac{3(s-s_0)}{s_1-s_0}-1\\right).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose a closed rational axis-aligned rectangle \\(J\\) contains the entire desired motion of an active rectangle, lies in the open unit square, and is disjoint from all other current rectangles.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) be one third of the minimum of the sup-norm distances from \\(J\\) to the other current rectangles and to the chart boundary, omitting the other-rectangle distances when there are no other rectangles.</p><p class=\"li\">For each coordinate interval \\([a,b]\\) of \\(J\\), use the factor\n  \\[\n  \\beta\\!\\left(\\frac{u-a+\\delta}{\\delta/2}\\right)\n  \\beta\\!\\left(\\frac{b+\\delta-u}{\\delta/2}\\right).\n  \\]\n  Let \\(\\chi\\) be the product of the two coordinate factors.</p><p class=\"li\">For a scalar function \\(G\\), define\n  \\[\n  V(s,X,Y)=\n  \\theta'(s)\\bigl(\\partial_Y(\\chi G),-\\partial_X(\\chi G)\\bigr), \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n  extending its compactly supported potential periodically.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The cutoff \\(\\chi\\) equals one near \\(J\\), is supported in the open chart, and vanishes near every stationary rectangle. The pulse is smooth, divergence free, has zero planar mean, and vanishes near the ends of its phase slot.</p><p>For translation by \\(\\mathbf u=(u_1,u_2)\\), taking\n\\[\nG=u_1Y-u_2X\n\\]\nmoves every point \\(\\mathbf w\\) of the active rectangle exactly along\n\\[\n\\mathbf w+\\theta(s)\\mathbf u.\n\\]</p><p>For scaling by \\(\\operatorname{diag}(\\mu,\\mu^{-1})\\), with \\(\\mu&gt;0\\), about a center \\(\\boldsymbol\\pi\\), taking\n\\[\nG=(\\log\\mu)(X-\\pi_1)(Y-\\pi_2)\n\\]\nmoves every point \\(\\mathbf w\\) of the active rectangle exactly along\n\\[\n\\boldsymbol\\pi+\n\\operatorname{diag}\\bigl(\\mu^{\\theta(s)},\\mu^{-\\theta(s)}\\bigr)\n(\\mathbf w-\\boldsymbol\\pi).\n\\]</p><p>These formulas hold whenever the swept bounding box lies in \\(J\\). Every other current rectangle stays fixed. All required cutoff and pulse derivative bounds are effective.</p>", "proof": "<p>The function \\(\\theta\\) interpolates from zero to one and is constant near both ends of the slot. The cutoff factors are one on a neighborhood of the coordinate intervals of \\(J\\) and supported in their \\(\\delta\\)-enlargements. The choice of \\(\\delta\\) keeps the support inside the chart and away from all stationary rectangles.</p><p>The field in (10) is Hamiltonian, so it is divergence free. Its components are derivatives of a periodic potential, so each has zero planar mean.</p><p>Differentiate each displayed path. Since \\(\\chi=1\\) on a neighborhood of every tracked point, the derivative agrees with the pulse field there. Smooth ODE uniqueness identifies the paths with the pulse flow. Other current rectangles stay fixed because the pulse vanishes near them.</p><p>The cutoff effectiveness and derivative bounds follow from Proposition 1; the remaining formulas use finite arithmetic, differentiation, and \\(\\log\\mu\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Collision-free routing and neighborhood action.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Collision-free routing and neighborhood action.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the rectangles \\(D_r,H_r\\), centers \\(\\mathbf d_r,\\mathbf h_r\\), branch maps \\(F_r\\), matrices \\(A_r\\), and rational \\(\\lambda\\) of Lemma 5.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Place parking centers at\n  \\[\n  \\boldsymbol\\pi_r=\n  \\left(\\frac12+\\frac{r}{4(N+1)},\\frac34\\right),\n  \\qquad 1\\leq r\\leq N.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Divide one phase period into \\(5N\\) equal slots and perform:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. In decreasing order of \\(\\mathbf d_{r,1}\\), translate \\(D_r\\) horizontally right to its parking column and then vertically to \\(\\boldsymbol\\pi_r\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. With all rectangles parked, apply \\(A_r\\) at each parking center, in index order.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. In increasing order of \\(\\mathbf h_{r,1}\\), translate each parked rectangle vertically to its target height and then horizontally left into \\(H_r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Break ties in the two orders by branch index. Implement each slot by Lemma 5.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every slot has a rational swept bounding box inside the open unit square, at positive distance from all stationary rectangles. The resulting one-period motion maps every full source rectangle according to \\(F_r\\).</p><p>There is an effectively computable \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) such that the period map agrees with \\(F_r\\) on the \\(\\rho\\)-neighborhood of every \\(D_r\\). The construction joins smoothly at all slot and phase endpoints and extends periodically.</p><p>Every tracked path lies in\n\\[\n1/4-\\lambda\\leq X\\leq3/4+\\lambda/2,\n\\qquad\n1/16\\leq Y\\leq3/4+\\lambda/2,\n\\]\nand hence in \\(J_*\\). If both the source and target states are nonterminal, then throughout the period\n\\[\n1/4-\\lambda\\leq Y\\leq3/4+\\lambda/2. \\tag{11}\n\\]\nThe terminal square lies in \\(H\\), at distance at least \\(1/32\\) from its boundary.</p>", "proof": "<p>The parking-column spacing is larger than \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p>During horizontal extraction, consider an unextracted rectangle whose vertical interval meets the active one. Since source rectangles are disjoint, their horizontal intervals are strictly separated. The decreasing-center order forces the other interval to lie wholly to the left; equal centers would force disjoint vertical intervals. It therefore cannot meet the rightward swept box. Previously parked rectangles lie near height \\(3/4\\), whereas sources lie below \\(1/2\\).</p><p>The subsequent vertical move is in a parking column, disjoint from the source squares near \\(X=1/4\\) and from the other parking columns.</p><p>During a scaling, each side length is between its initial and final lengths, both at most \\(\\lambda\\). The entire motion therefore lies in\n\\[\n\\boldsymbol\\pi_r+[-\\lambda/2,\\lambda/2]^2,\n\\]\nseparated from the other parking boxes.</p><p>A vertical insertion uses an empty parking column and avoids the state squares. During horizontal insertion, parked rectangles are above target heights. Any previously inserted target whose vertical interval meets the active target has its horizontal interval wholly to the left, by disjointness and the increasing-center order. It therefore does not meet the active rectangle’s leftward swept box, which stops at its own target.</p><p>All swept bounding boxes lie inside the open unit square. Their endpoints, including those of the scaling bounding boxes, are rational, and their distances to stationary boxes are positive. Lemma 5.2 applies at every slot. Induction through the slots proves that each full source rectangle follows its assigned map, while all other tracked rectangles remain fixed during that pulse.</p><p>For neighborhood action, include two kinds of clearance at every slot:</p><p class=\"li\">the distance of the swept active rectangle from the complement of the region where its cutoff is one;</p><p class=\"li\">the distance of each held rectangle from the support of that cutoff.</p><p>The cutoff recipe gives effective positive lower bounds for both. Minimize them over the finite set of slots and tracked rectangles, obtaining \\(m_0&gt;0\\). Let \\(M\\geq1\\) bound the operator norm of every partial affine product along every branch, using the identity during a held slot. Such a bound is computed from the finitely many translations and reciprocal scalings. Choose rational\n\\[\n0&lt;\\rho&lt;\\frac{m_0}{2M}.\n\\]\nInduction through the slots shows that a perturbation of norm less than \\(\\rho\\) stays in the active one-plateau or inactive zero-region, as appropriate, and follows the same affine formulas. All sources have been parked before target insertion starts, so source–target overlaps introduce no additional simultaneous obstacles.</p><p>The pulses vanish near slot joints and phase endpoints. Joining and repeating them periodically gives a smooth field.</p><p>The tracked-path bounds follow from the state-square locations, parking locations, and the fact that translations interpolate endpoint intervals. The only scaling occurs at height \\(3/4\\). For nonterminal source and target states, (6) gives (11), including held portions of the trajectory. Since \\(\\lambda&lt;1/100\\), its lower bound exceeds \\(3/16\\).</p><p>The terminal square has\n\\[\n1/16\\leq Y\\leq1/16+\\lambda.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\lambda&lt;1/32\\), it lies in \\(H\\) at distance at least \\(1/32\\) from the boundary.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**An autonomous mean-zero spatial clock.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**An autonomous mean-zero spatial clock.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V(s,X,Y)\\) be the planar processor of Proposition 5 for a fixed machine and input.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  J_*=[1/8,7/8]\\times[1/32,7/8].\n  \\]\n  Choose an effective smooth cutoff \\(\\chi_*(X,Y)\\), equal to one near \\(J_*\\) and compactly supported in the open unit-square chart.</p><p class=\"li\">Extend compactly supported products periodically and define\n  \\[\n  g(X,Y)=\\partial_X\\bigl(X\\chi_*(X,Y)\\bigr),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  W(X,Y,Z)=\n  \\bigl(V_1(Z,X,Y),V_2(Z,X,Y),g(X,Y)\\bigr). \\tag{12}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  P=(1/4,1/4,0),\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal O=\\{(X,Y,Z):1/32&lt;Y&lt;1/8\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The field \\(W\\) is computably smooth, periodic, divergence free, and mean zero. Every spatial derivative has an effective finite bound. Moreover,\n\\[\ng=1\n\\]\non a neighborhood of \\(J_*\\).</p><p>The autonomous \\(W\\)-flow is globally defined. Its trajectory from \\(P\\) encodes the compiled run at integer path-parameter values, up to terminal entry if it occurs, and enters \\(\\mathcal O\\) at a finite path-parameter value exactly when the original machine halts.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Proposition 1 to construct \\(\\chi_*\\) with rational margins. The formula defines a smooth periodic field with effective derivative bounds.</p><p>Its divergence vanishes because \\(V\\) has zero planar divergence and \\(g\\) is independent of \\(Z\\). Each component has zero spatial mean by its derivative representation. Since \\(\\chi_*=1\\) near \\(J_*\\),\n\\[\ng=\\partial_XX=1\n\\]\nthere. It need not be positive on the whole torus.</p><p>For a computational planar segment starting at phase zero, the path\n\\[\ns\\longmapsto\\bigl(\\Phi_s^V(X,Y),\\,s\\bmod1\\bigr),\n\\qquad 0\\leq s\\leq1,\n\\]\nsolves the autonomous \\(W\\)-equation: its planar component stays in \\(J_*\\), where \\(g=1\\).</p><p>The smooth field has a unique global flow. Periodicity gives boundedness and a uniform spatial Lipschitz constant, so its local ODE solution continues through every finite time. Uniqueness identifies the displayed path with that flow.</p><p>Repeat this argument period by period. The trajectory from \\(P\\) encodes the compiled run at integer path-parameter values, up to and including terminal entry if it occurs. No absorbing motion after terminal entry is required for the existential event. Proposition 5 gives all-time exclusion in the nonhalting case and terminal inclusion in the halting case, proving the equivalence.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [0, 3, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 10, "cite"], [4, 12, "cite"], [4, 14, "cite"], [5, 0, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 1, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 1, "cite"], [10, 14, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [11, 13, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 10, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [14, 2, "cite"]]});
